{"tool_name":"phagesdb","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"phagesdb","home":"http://phagesdb.org","summary":"The Actinobacteriophage Database.","addition_date":"2022-09-30T22:49:20.141109Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T15:32:44.704672Z","tool_type":["Database portal"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:phagesdb","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"gfh@pitt.edu","name":"Graham F. Hatfull","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6705-6821","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Peptide immunogenicity prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0252"}]}],"name":"phagesdb","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Jennifer","publication":[{"doi":"10.1371/journal.ppat.1010602","metadata":{"abstract":"AMUyc:oPblaecatseercioonpfhiramgtehsa-talblhaecatdeirniogplehvaeglseasreirnefpercetsinegntMedyccoorrbeacctltye:rium hosts-contribute substantially to our understanding of viral diversity and evolution, provide resources for advancing Mycobacterium genetics, are the basis of high-impact science education programs, and show considerable therapeutic potential. Over 10,000 individual mycobacteriophages have been isolated by high school and undergraduate students using the model organism Mycobacterium smegmatis mc2155 and 2,100 have been completely sequenced, giving a highresolution view of the phages that infect a single common host strain. The phage genomes are revealed to be highly diverse and architecturally mosaic and are replete with genes of unknown function. Mycobacteriophages have provided many widely used tools for Mycobacterium genetics including integration-proficient vectors and recombineering systems, as well as systems for efficient delivery of reporter genes, transposons, and allelic exchange substrates. The genomic insights and engineering tools have facilitated exploration of phages for treatment of Mycobacterium infections, although their full therapeutic potential has yet to be realized.","authors":[{"name":"Hatfull G.F."}],"citationCount":40,"date":"2022-07-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"PLoS Pathogens","title":"Mycobacteriophages: From Petri dish to patient"},"pmcid":"PMC9262239","pmid":"35797343"}],"topic":[{"term":"Evolutionary biology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3299"},{"term":"Genomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0622"},{"term":"Mapping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0102"},{"term":"Microbiology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3301"},{"term":"Mobile genetic elements","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0798"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"phagesdb","home":"https://bio.tools/phagesdb","summary":"The Actinobacteriophage Database.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:phagesdb/db/phagesdb.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":59,"citations":[{"count":3,"year":"2022"},{"count":8,"year":"2026"},{"count":13,"year":"2024"},{"count":17,"year":"2023"},{"count":18,"year":"2025"}],"doi":"10.1371/journal.ppat.1010602","pmcid":"PMC9262239","pmid":"35797343","ref_count":124,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"1960"},{"count":1,"year":"1970"},{"count":1,"year":"1978"},{"count":1,"year":"1979"},{"count":1,"year":"1989"},{"count":1,"year":"1991"},{"count":1,"year":"1999"},{"count":1,"year":"2004"},{"count":1,"year":"2007"},{"count":1,"year":"2023"},{"count":2,"year":"-1"},{"count":2,"year":"1992"},{"count":2,"year":"1995"},{"count":2,"year":"1997"},{"count":2,"year":"2001"},{"count":2,"year":"2005"},{"count":2,"year":"2010"},{"count":2,"year":"2016"},{"count":3,"year":"1998"},{"count":3,"year":"2000"},{"count":3,"year":"2013"},{"count":4,"year":"2003"},{"count":4,"year":"2009"},{"count":4,"year":"2011"},{"count":4,"year"